      "rationale": "📖️ A third-party READER of the DWG file header, run as a separate process and never linked, so its GPL-3.0 licence stays with the tool. `dwgread -v3` traces the preamble fields it parses — the version code, `maint_version` and `codepage` — before it decodes the R2004+ section map, and refuses a document shorter than a container with the byte count it was given. The case's oracle role writes every document it produces to disk and requires that reading to agree with the semantic-dwg-preamble-v1 projection (`dwgread_agrees` in `../🔮️oracles/🦀️.rs`); the mutation itself is applied by the independently hand-written preamble writer beside it. Verified on the committed architectural drawing (AC1024, maint 2, codepage 30) and on AC1032/maint 7/codepage 29 and 22-byte preamble-only documents. Installed on macOS through Homebrew (`libredwg`); Ubuntu 24.04 and Windows ship no package, so there the oracle phase records the case as oracle-unavailable with the missing command."
    }
  ],
  "noOracleDecisions": [],
  "comparisonProfiles": [
    {
      "id": "semantic-dwg-preamble-v1",
      "description": "DWG documents compared by the publicly specified preamble — the six-character version string, the maintenance-version byte and the codepage — plus the document's own byte length. `byteLength` is NORMATIVE here, not writer freedom: the rest of an R2004+ container is a proprietary section map that neither side can rebuild, so the length is the only way a whole-document replacement is observable at all, and neither side has any latitude over it. Nothing beyond the preamble is projected, because nothing beyond the preamble can be read independently of this repository's own decoder; a profile that claimed more would be claiming evidence that does not exist.",
      "tolerance": 0,
      "ignoreKeys": []
    }
  ],
